<blockquote>
Leading the Masses: Planning for the Future<br />
Written by <a href="mailto:mayhem@conclaveofguilds.com">Mayhem</a><br />
</br>
Welcome to the first edition of the Conclave of Guilds site's Leading the
Masses column.  In this inaugural edition, we're taking a look at what it
takes to start from nothing, literally, and plan out a successful guild
which will take off (we hope) like a rocket upon the launch of Everquest 2.
June being four, five, six or even more months away from the game's release,
you may ask why even bother thinking about it now.  Or perhaps even what
possible preparations could you make anyway?  Read on, and your curiosity
will hopefully be rewarded.<br />
<br />
The biggest initial question is: "why now?"  After all, with months left to
go until the release of this highly anticipated sequel to the biggest MMORPG
ever, it would seem a bit premature.  However, that's just a surface
perception, and not an adequate look into the situation at hand.<br />
<br />
What we have coming up is the release of a game which promises to be so big
it will dwarf the previous (massively popular) title in subscribers, depth,
and content.  Don't believe the hype?  I don't blame you.  Few people felt
that Everquest would become as popular as it has, and for a long period of
time in the latter 90's (such as when Everquest was being developed) there
was extreme doubt as to the viability of online-only games for mass-market
consumption.<br />
<br />
Let's lay the case out quickly, shall we?  Everquest 2 takes many of the
familiar elements of it's hugely successful parent, then restructures them
to make them somewhat fresh.  It focuses more greatly on allowing newer
players to get into the action comfortably, while ensuring that old hands
are not put off by slow and boring requirements at the start.  It
capitalizes on new design features which address the desires of the
Everquest player base, as well as truly designing the game for ease of
expansion.  Staff working on Everquest 2 have admitted to the poorly
designed expansion of the original, indicating that while they never
expected to truly need to expand the original game to the extent that they
have, EQ2 has been built from the bottom up with that expandability in mind.<br />
<br />
What does all of this add up to?  A game which will be so massive within the
first year of it's release that it will cause gaming companies to re-think
their strategies in years to come. (Mind you, I'm not usually into
predicting industry trends, but I'm pretty confident on this one.) You have
a more powerful, user-friendly, and expandable game than the one which
earned the ever-apt nickname "Evercrack" . . . and you as a potential guild
leader are in a position to be on the leading edge of those charging "once
more into the breach" as it were.<br />
<br />
Excited yet?  If not, just remember that guilds will have an opportunity to
purchase (in an undisclosed manner) real estate in Everquest 2.  That's
right, with enough money, a guild can have a prime piece of land in one of
the major cities.  This isn't like Ultima Online where just getting a place
to lay down a castle and add to the ever-spreading urban-decay look was what
passed for "cool" at the time.  No, this is truly limited space, and from
all indications, it's likely to be first come first serve.  Those guilds
with the coordination and discipline to get their act together and make
payments (and presumably keep making them, as I doubt very much SOE would
pass up the opportunity to charge exorbitant rent) on the prime land will be
the ones with the banners flying, and some of the best in-game advertising
virtual platinum can buy.<br />
<br />
So, having (hopefully) established firmly in your mind the reasons behind
getting a jump on the competition, we should move on to a bit about what you
can do about it at this point.  First off, your benefit in planning ahead
will become apparent in the speed with which you will be able to get the
guild up and running once things finally fall into place and the game opens
up.  Less time arranging things needed by a successful guild means more time
in game getting to the point where your guild can take it's rightful place
amongst the "powers that be" on your server.<br />
<br />
There are a number of things to think about when starting up a new guild.
Each of them bears a good deal of attention, and you shouldn't be tempted to
leave any of them to the last moment, lest what you come up with in the
final rush be sloppy and cumbersome.  The point of starting to think about
this so soon is to avoid that.  Don't handicap yourself by only thinking
about the easiest parts of forming a guild.  (Whatever those may be for you,
as each individual is going to find things more or less difficult, according
to their skills.)<br />
<br />
Probably the first determination you need to make is simple, yet difficult.
Should you really be running a guild?  Seriously now, just thinking it would
be cool to lead a guild is probably the number one reason people try to
start them, and the number one reason they fall apart.  Being a Guild Leader
is a lot of responsibility, hard work, and difficult decisions.  This is
balanced by the fact that you will hopefully get a good deal of satisfaction
out of your relationships with your guildmates, and know that the work you
do is benefiting your community.  Thus, being a Guild Leader can be
described as being willing to do extra work in order to ensure that the
players around you have a more successful and pleasurable experience.  Guild
leadership is a social responsibility, not a mantle to be worn as it pleases
you.  It's also a matter of working with people, both inside and outside of
your guild, and bringing a dependable and intelligent presence to these
interactions.<br />
<br />
So, ask yourself some questions before going any further.  Are you willing
to put your in-game time second to dealing with guild business at times?
Can you set aside time outside of the game in order to handle guild
business?  Do you have the strength of character to persevere even when
times are rough within your guild?  Are you able to delegate tasks to other
members (such as officers and guildmates who provide support outside the
game) while maintaining enough contact with them to be sure that processes
run smoothly?  If these questions come up "yes", you should move on.  If
not, there's no shame in it, because not everyone is suited to lead a guild.
If you still have the desire to provide some of the services and/or (most
important of all) support that a guild needs, by all means take some time to
get involved in the early stages of a guild which is forming!<br />
<br />
Can you afford to run a guild?  This is a legitimate question which needs a
good, honest answer.  It is something you should consider right after
determining if running a guild is for you.  Running a top-notch guild will
cost money in terms of web-hosting costs.  While there are "free" sites
which provide many of the hosting services that a guild needs, the absolute
best solution is to have your own web space and domain.  This allows you (or
your web support staff, as many Guild Leaders delegate these duties) to have
much better control over the content, appearance, and services you provide
to your guild and the community it interacts with.  In terms of financial
outlay this will usually be between $15 to $40 to start up, and $4 to $20 on
a monthly basis.  Essentially you're looking at paying a monthly amount
which will on average be equal to what you pay to play Everquest 2.  This
does not take into account any programs which you may wish to buy or
register in order to upkeep the guild.  These programs can range from $5 up
into the hundreds of dollars depending on their capabilities.  The best
advice which can be given regarding these programs is to really think about
what will give the guild the most benefit, while costing the least.  In the
end, you cannot assume that guildmates will be forthcoming in assisting you
with the financial requirements of running the guild.  While this does
happen at times, more often than not, the Guild Leader ends up footing the
bill.  (When it's someone other than the Guild Leader, it usually ends up
being the designated webmaster.)<br />
<br />
Find some like-minded individuals.  First, remember that simply wanting to
play EQ2 and being there does not make someone a potentially good guildmate
. . . make sure that you include people who have a specific qualities you're
looking for in guildmates.  If possible, play some games online with them,
perhaps even starting a low-level group in Everquest (if you all still play
and have the inclination) just to get a feel for personalities.  Be honest
about your intentions, and assure potential candidates that you just want a
group of people who can work and play well together.  You don't need a lot,
just some of them who are looking towards the release of Everquest 2 and
share your desire (as well as the aforementioned "x factor" qualities) to
get started early.  There are plenty of people out there, you just need to
scour the Everquest, Everquest 2, and other MMORPG communities to discover
allies in your plan to launch an impressive guild.  Of course, if you are
already part of an Everquest guild, some of your fellow guildmates might be
planning the same move you are, making it even easier to acquire assistance.
In some respects, this will become easier as the release date comes closer,
and as the game goes into Beta.  If at all possible, make sacrifices
(buckets of KFC anyone?) to the gods and pray for one of your fellows (or
even yourself) to be accepted to the beta program. This will not really
assist you in terms of game knowledge (NDAs in place will prevent sharing of
information until very late Beta, even amongst friends and relatives), but
it will give that person access to the valuable human resource . . . and
they can act as an in-Beta recruiter for your organization.<br />
<br />
Much like the founding fathers of whatever country you reside in, you're
going to need laws to govern your personal pocket-empire.  Drafting a
charter is far too complex to go into within this article, but you can look
forward to one covering this topic in-depth soon.  The most important thing
to remember is that the charter is not just something to pay lip service.
It should reflect the attitudes and restrictions of your guild.  A
well-designed charter will give potential new members information they need
to regarding your guild's policies and procedures.  It will also serve to
give other guilds or individuals an idea of how your guild works, thus
allowing them a better chance at approaching your guild regarding various
situations which may arise.  (Such as the always-dreaded and ever-recurring
complaint against a member of your guild.)<br />
<br />
Determine who is going to be in charge of the web development for your
guild.  Make sure it is someone who has enough knowledge that they can
effectively implement the guild's needs, or enough time to learn the skills
they lack.  Make sure that you have discussions about the needs and desires
of your guildmates.  A web site is primarily used to provide information and
resources to the guild members.  Secondarily it provides a point of contact
for those interested in your guild, or who represent other guilds (or just
themselves) from your server.  Key aspects of a guild web site may be
categorized as: guild news, member contact information, guild forums, and
general guild information.  These four main categories are important for
both members and non-members alike.  By determining the needs of your
members, you can adequately provide information.  During the development and
upkeep of the web site, be aware of the updated desires of your guild
members.  A static web site does little good, so be sure that whoever is in
charge of development is adequately updating the content.  Fewer people want
to join a guild whose last news update was a month ago when the other guilds
on the server have updated within the last week.  (To be honest, we all know
that the single greatest factor in people wanting to join a guild is success
and chemistry in-game, but a strong web presence never hurt.)  When
designing the site, pay close attention to "members-only" areas.  These
kinds of backrooms can contain much more information than you may want the
general public to know, but are still vital to the development of your
guild.<br />
<br />
Spending time in regular meetings to discuss the guild's progress and plans
is vital, even pre-release.  Get together with your founding members and
discuss the guild, as well as the progress of the game itself.  Share
information and ideas, as well as hopes and dreams.  Doing this kind of
meeting in a chat environment is ideal, so try to arrange a time and medium
which is suitable for as many people as possible.  Get a log of the chat for
those people who were not able to make it, and open up a post in your forums
dedicated to what was discussed during the meeting.  These kinds of meetings
are ideal for getting feedback on the progress of the web site, of
recruitment efforts (such as any which may be ongoing during Beta), and
other business which may be going on.  The frequency of such chats should be
dictated by the needs of your project, but once a month would probably be a
good balance for most guilds, particularly when combined with a good set of
forums.<br />
<br />
Finally, once the "big day" draws near, you need to finalize your plans.
Serious gamers who have been waiting months (or years) for this release will
be spending a large amount of time playing from the word "go" . . . while
more casual gamers will merely be in a hurry to get it installed so they can
start to gently probe as they always have.  No matter what style you and
your fellows have, it is important to think about how things need to flow
upon release.  If your goal is to have a guild capable of buying that
expensive and prestigious real estate, you're going to have to work for it.
Get some idea as to who is planning on playing what character and where in
the world they're going to start. Many people will have made decisions based
upon information only available during (a possible) public Beta, or simply
from increased coverage near launch.  Creating balanced groups would seem to
be important in Everquest 2 (which comes as no surprise to those who have
played plenty of the original) and starting off with a good mix of planned
(since everyone starts out as a no-class of their race) character types will
serve the whole guild.  Of course, as soon as the servers are announced
(which could be the first time one of your members logs in) you'll need to
make the decision as to which one to join . . . and make sure everyone
knows!<br />
<br />
Once Everquest 2 is launched the chaos really starts.  Keep in mind all the
preparations you've made and rely on that infrastructure you've spent your
time building.  Then remember that no matter how much there seems to be done
in order to make your guild "official" in-game, you're better off than those
people who realized the week before release that the mother of all
"next-gen" MMORPGs was upon them.  Get in there . . . explore, experience,
and enjoy the freedom that taking care of all the "little details"
beforehand has given you.<br />
<br />
For convenience, here's a quick list of the steps outlined above:<br />
<ul>
<li>
Should I run a guild?
</li>
<li>
Can I afford to run the guild?
</li>
<li>
Attract the hordes!
</li>
<li>
Draft your own "Declaration of Independence".
</li>
<li>
Get your web presence up and running.
</li>
<li>
Keep the spirit alive during pre-release.
</li>
<li>
Make those last minute adjustments.
</li>
<li>
Take over the world!  (Or die trying...)
</li>
</ul>
Next time in Leading the Masses we're planning to take a look at designing a
charter which suits your guild.  So be sure to check back for updates!  We're
aiming to make the Everquest 2 Conclave of Guilds your premier spot for all
guild news now and throughout the run of the game!<br />
<br />
</blockquote>
